---
import EnglishHeader from '../../../components/EnglishHeader.astro';
import EnglishFooter from '../../../components/EnglishFooter.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const images = `${base}/images/bikes/rossin-record`;
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="1978 Rossin Record: an early Italian Columbus SL racing bicycle with Campagnolo Record, pantographed details and yellow-and-blue paint."/><meta property="og:type" content="article"/><meta property="og:title" content="Rossin Record 1978 · The Evolution of Bikes"/><meta property="og:description" content="An early Rossin racer combining Columbus SL, Campagnolo Record and the marque’s own distinctive details."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/bikes/rossin-record/hero.webp"/><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"/><title>Rossin Record 1978 · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head>
<body><EnglishHeader current="bikes" danishPath="/cykler/rossin-record/"/><main class="atala-page">
  <header class="quick-hero"><p class="section-label">In the collection · Cavenago Brianza, Italy · 1978</p><h1>Rossin<br/><em>Record</em></h1><p>An early professional racer from a young Italian marque, built in Columbus SL and made recognisable by colour, chrome and a name repeated all the way down into the metal.</p></header>
  <figure class="quick-pass"><img src={`${images}/hero.webp`} alt="Yellow and blue 1978 Rossin Record viewed from the drive side"/><figcaption>The 1978 Rossin Record. Its yellow-to-blue finish, chrome fork and personalised components define the marque’s early appearance. Photo: The Evolution of Bikes.</figcaption></figure>
  <section class="atala-facts" aria-label="Key facts"><div><strong>1978</strong><span>Stated year</span></div><div><strong>Columbus SL</strong><span>Light Italian steel</span></div><div><strong>Campagnolo</strong><span>Record build</span></div><div><strong>Rossin</strong><span>Established in 1974</span></div></section>
  <section class="atala-opening"><p class="section-label">A name in the details</p><p>Rossin appears on more than the down tube. The name is repeated on the stem, chainset and toe straps, while the angular R returns at the head tube and brakes. This was a bicycle designed to be recognised at close range as well as from across the road.</p></section>
  <section class="atala-story"><div><p class="section-label">A young marque</p><h2>From Colnago to a workshop of his own</h2></div><div><p>Rossin was established in 1974 by Mario Rossin and five partners. Mario Rossin had previously worked at Colnago and wanted to build his own marque around precision, form and speed.</p><p>The Record was among its early professional models. Rossin later became known for experiments with internal cable routing, aerodynamic tube shapes and new geometries. This bicycle, however, begins with a classic lightweight steel construction.</p></div></section>
  <section class="atala-details"><figure><img src={`${images}/cockpit.webp`} alt="Rossin-pantographed stem and handlebar" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The Rossin name on the stem and Italian colours around the head tube.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${images}/headtube.webp`} alt="Head tube, Campagnolo down-tube shifters and chrome fork crown" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The inset R, profiled lug and finely patterned gear levers.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${images}/saddle.webp`} alt="San Marco Laser saddle and pantographed seatpost" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The presently fitted San Marco Laser saddle and seatpost with Italian colours.</figcaption></figure></section>
  <section class="atala-components"><div><p class="section-label">The Record build</p><h2>A catalogue bicycle with its own signature</h2></div><div><p>The period Rossin catalogue describes the Record with Columbus SL tubing and Campagnolo Record gears and brakes. Nisi Solidal or Ambrosio Synthesis rims, ACI spokes, Regina Oro chain and freewheel, and a 3TTT bar and stem completed the factory proposal.</p><p>The surviving bicycle follows the same idea, but a bicycle from 1978 may have received replacement parts over the years. The catalogue specification is therefore a historical comparison, not a guarantee that every fitted component remains original.</p></div></section>
  <section class="atala-detail-pair"><figure><img src={`${images}/drivetrain.webp`} alt="Pantographed Rossin chainset and Campagnolo front derailleur" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The Rossin lettering and R emblems turn standard components into part of a coherent design.</figcaption></figure><div><p class="section-label">Columbus SL</p><h2>Light steel before aero tubing</h2><p>Columbus SL was a natural choice for an Italian top model in the late 1970s. Its thin-walled tubes offered a light frame, while the lugs and chrome surfaces left room for a carefully finished construction.</p><p>The blue and yellow paint moves across the joints in soft transitions. Together with the pantographing, it is the kind of work that made Rossin bicycles easy to distinguish from more anonymous catalogue frames.</p></div></section>
  <section class="atala-details"><figure><img src={`${images}/catalogue.webp`} alt="Historical Rossin Record catalogue page with bicycle and specification" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The Rossin Record catalogue shows the Columbus SL frame and the factory’s proposed Campagnolo Record build. Source: period Rossin catalogue, collection archive.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${images}/tubing-decal.webp`} alt="Worn Columbus SL tubing decal on the Rossin Record" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The surviving Columbus SL decal documents the tubing directly on the frame.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${images}/shifters.webp`} alt="Campagnolo gear levers and Rossin detail on the down tube" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>The gear levers and small R near the head tube bring mechanics and graphics together.</figcaption></figure></section>
  <section class="koga-specs"><div><p class="section-label">The bicycle in the collection</p><h2>Brief specification</h2></div><dl><div><dt>Model</dt><dd>Rossin Record</dd></div><div><dt>Year</dt><dd>1978, stated</dd></div><div><dt>Frame</dt><dd>Lugged Columbus SL steel</dd></div><div><dt>Gears and brakes</dt><dd>Campagnolo Record</dd></div><div><dt>Defining details</dt><dd>Pantographed components, chrome and yellow-and-blue paint</dd></div><div><dt>Saddle</dt><dd>San Marco Laser, presently fitted</dd></div><div><dt>Status</dt><dd>In the collection</dd></div></dl></section>
  <section class="atala-story"><div><p class="section-label">Sources</p><h2>The basis of this page</h2></div><div><p>The model and its original build have been compared with a period Rossin Record catalogue page held in the collection archive. The date, provenance and information about Mario Rossin also draw on the documentation supplied with the bicycle.</p><p>The present specification is assessed from the bicycle and the detail photographs shown here. Where the catalogue and the surviving bicycle may differ, the distinction is made in the text.</p></div></section>
  <section class="atala-closing"><p>The Rossin Record shows a marque early in its development: the classic Columbus frame is in place, while the colour, engraving and small R emblems already point to a maker determined to leave a signature of its own.</p><div class="bike-bottom-nav"><a href={`${base}/en/bikes/`}>← Back to bicycles</a><a href={`${base}/en/periods/1970-1979/`}>View the period 1970–1979 →</a></div></section>
</main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
